# Questions and Answers (Correct Answers Bolded)

**Question 1:**  
What role does a sample population play in determining uniqueness constraints?  
A. It is irrelevant for constraints  
B. It defines attribute data types  
C. **It helps identify which combinations are unique**  
D. It ensures the database is normalized  

**Question 2:**  
What type of relationship exists when each politician is born in one country, but a country can be the birthplace of multiple politicians?  
A. One-to-one  
B. **Many-to-one**  
C. One-to-many  
D. Many-to-many  

**Question 3:**  
Can a fact type have multiple uniqueness constraints?  
A. Only for ternary or higher fact types  
B. **No, only one is allowed**  
C. Only for binary fact types  
D. Yes, if they do not conflict  

**Question 4:**  
In ORM diagrams, how is a uniqueness constraint visually represented?  
A. With an arrow  
B. With a circle  
C. **With a bar over the role(s)**  
D. With a diamond  

**Question 5:**  
What type of relationship is represented when each person has only one mother?  
A. One-to-many  
B. Many-to-many  
C. **Many-to-one**  
D. One-to-one  

**Question 6:**  
In a ternary fact type involving Person, Subject, and Rating, what does a uniqueness constraint on Person and Subject imply?  
A. Each rating is unique  
B. **Each person-subject pair has at most one rating**  
C. Each person can have only one subject  
D. Each subject can have only one rating  

**Question 7:**  
What is a common approach to uniquely identify entities when natural attributes are insufficient?  
A. Normalize the database  
B. Add more attributes  
C. **Use a surrogate key, such as an ID number**  
D. Use a composite key  

**Question 8:**  
How does uniqueness in data modeling differ from uniqueness in reality?  
A. **Data modeling uses attributes, reality uses the entity’s essence**  
B. They are identical  
C. Data modeling uniqueness is optional, reality is mandatory  
D. Data modeling enforces uniqueness via the database, reality via inherent properties  

**Question 9:**  
In a ternary fact type, what does a uniqueness constraint on two roles imply?  
A. The entire row is unique  
B. Each value in the first role is unique  
C. **Each combination of those two roles is unique**  
D. Each value in the second role is unique